2 \\end{matrix}\\right]\\frac{1}{(n-k)!}\n+\n\\frac{1}{n-k}\\sum_{j=1}^{k-1}\\frac{1}{j}\\left(\\begin{matrix} n-k-1+j \\\\ j \\end{matrix}\\right)\n\\right).\n\\]\n\n*Proof.* This can be obtained by hand from the formula on Equation (4.10).--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nSkew shapes, Ehrhart positivity, and beyond - Ferroni - 2026 - Proceedings of the London Mathematical Society - Wiley Online Library (https://londmathsoc.onlinelibrary.wiley.com/doi/10.1112/plms.70204)\nciteturn1search1 [wordlim: 200] Published: 2 months ago; Crawled: last week; In [34, Appendix A] Ferroni and Schröter proved that direct sums of snake matroids, loops, and coloops, span the valuative group of matroids introduced by Eur et al. ... As a consequence of Corollary 5.6, we observe the following property of Ehrhart polynomials of matroid base polytopes: they are nonnegative on a spanning set of the valuative group, yet they fail to be nonnegative in general due to the counterexamples found in [29].\n[26]. In other words, the Ehrhart polynomial of every matroid can be written as an integer combination of Ehrhart polynomials of matroids that are direct sums of snakes, loops, and coloops. As a consequence of Corollary 5.6, we observe the following property of Ehrhart polynomials of matroid base polytopes: they are nonnegative on a spanning set of the valuative group, yet they fail to be nonnegative in general due to the counterexamples found in [29]. To the best of the authors' knowledge, this is the first valuative invariant of matroids arising “in nature”§ for which this phenomenon occurs.\n\nWe close this section with one intriguing open problem. All snake matroids are series-parallel matroids. However, the converse is false, as can be seen by taking the graphic matroid induced by a 3-cycle in which every edge is doubled in parallel—that matroid is series-parallel, but not isomorphic to a lattice path matroid. We do not know how to extend the Ehrhart positivity of snakes to all series-parallel matroids.\n\nProblem 5.7.Show that all series-parallel matroids are Ehrhart positive.\n\nWe note that this is a special case of a conjecture by Ferroni et al. [33, Conjecture 6.2] on the Ehrhart positivity of positroids.\n\nRemark 5.8.From our main result, it follows that Corollary 5.6 admits the following upgrade: the Ehrhart polynomial of a matroid that is a direct sum of snakes and loops/coloops is nonnegative when seen as a polynomial in $$t-1$$. This property is not true for positroids. For example, the hypersimplex $$\\Delta _{2,9}$$ is a positroid, but its Ehrhart polynomial at $$t-1$$ has a negative cubic coefficient.\n\nRemark 5.9.After the completion of this paper, Chavez et al. [13] found a surprising application of the calculations carried out in Section 4. These authors were able to prove that the $$\\beta$$-invariant of any matroid without loops and coloops can be read off from the Ehrhart polynomial of the base polytope. It is utterly opaque to see the property proved in [13] from just the definition of the Ehrhart polynomial and the $$\\beta$$-invariant. Currently, the only known proof relies on Proposition 4.1 in an essential way.\n--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nMatroids are not Ehrhart positive (https://arxiv.org/abs/2105.04465)\nciteturn1academia13 [wordlim: 200] Published: 5.5 years ago; Authors: Luis Ferroni ... This allows us to give a precise expression for the Ehrhart polynomials of all sparse paving matroids, a class of matroids which is conjectured to be predominant and which contains the counterexamples arising from our construction.\nTitle: Matroids are not Ehrhart positive\nAuthors: Luis Ferroni\nDate: Mon May 10 16:00:43 2021\n\nIn this article we disprove the conjectures asserting the positivity of the coefficients of the Ehrhart polynomial of matroid polytopes by De Loera, Haws and K\\\"oppe (2007) and of generalized permutohedra by Castillo and Liu (2015). We prove constructively that for every $n\\geq 19$ there exist connected matroids on $n$ elements that are not Ehrhart positive. Also, we prove that for every $k\\geq 3$ there exist connected matroids of rank $k$ that are not Ehrhart positive. Our proofs rely on our previous results on the geometric interpretation of the operation of circuit-hyperplane relaxation and our formulas for the Ehrhart polynomials of hypersimplices and minimal matroids. This allows us to give a precise expression for the Ehrhart polynomials of all sparse paving matroids, a class of matroids which is conjectured to be predominant and which contains the counterexamples arising from our construction.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReal-rootedness and ultra log-concavity of rank-two matroid Ehrhart $h^*$-polynomials (https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.37439)\nciteturn1academia14 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Title: Real-rootedness and ultra log-concavity of rank-two matroid Ehrhart $h^*$-polynomials ... This bound is sharp: the rank-two matroids with parallel-class sizes $(4,561,600)$ and $(4,a,a+29)$, for all sufficiently large integers $a$, have $h^*$-polynomials that are not real-rooted. These counterexamples disprove Ferroni's real-rootedness conjecture.\nTitle: Real-rootedness and ultra log-concavity of rank-two matroid Ehrhart $h^*$-polynomials\nAuthors: Houshan Fu\nDate: Tue Sep 29 13:00:02 2026\n\nWe prove that the Ehrhart $h^*$-polynomial of a rank-two matroid with exactly three parallel classes is real-rooted whenever its smallest parallel class has size at most three. This bound is sharp: the rank-two matroids with parallel-class sizes $(4,561,600)$ and $(4,a,a+29)$, for all sufficiently large integers $a$, have $h^*$-polynomials that are not real-rooted. These counterexamples disprove Ferroni's real-rootedness conjecture. Their duals are cycle matroids of theta graphs and have the same $h^*$-polynomials. Nevertheless, every matroid of rank two or corank two has a positive $h^*$-coefficient sequence that is ultra log-concave of order equal to the polynomial's degree. In particular, the unimodality conjecture holds in both cases.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nReal-rootedness and ultra log-concavity of rank-two matroid Ehrhart $h^*$-polynomials - arXiv Combinatorics (https://www.arxiv.symmetricfunctions.com/paper/2609.37439v1)\nciteturn1search2 [wordlim: 200] Published: last month; Crawled: last month; ## Real-rootedness and ultra log-concavity of rank-two matroid Ehrhart $h^*$-polynomials ... This bound is sharp: the rank-two matroids with parallel-class sizes $(4,561,600)$ and $(4,a,a+29)$, for all sufficiently large integers $a$, have $h^*$-polynomials that are not real-rooted. These counterexamples disprove Ferroni's real-rootedness conjecture.\n\n## Real-rootedness and ultra log-concavity of rank-two matroid Ehrhart $h^*$-polynomials\n\nHoushan Fu\n\nPublished: 2026-09-29 | Updated: 2026-09-30\n\nComments: 21 pages\n\nNote:\n\nPublication:\n\nmath.CO\n\nehrhart polynomial graph log-concave matroid real-rooted sequence unimodality polynomial\n\n# • BIB • PDF • ARXIV •\n\n### Abstract\n\nWe prove that the Ehrhart $h^*$-polynomial of a rank-two matroid with exactly three parallel classes is real-rooted whenever its smallest parallel class has size at most three. This bound is sharp: the rank-two matroids with parallel-class sizes $(4,561,600)$ and $(4,a,a+29)$, for all sufficiently large integers $a$, have $h^*$-polynomials that are not real-rooted. These counterexamples disprove Ferroni's real-rootedness conjecture. Their duals are cycle matroids of theta graphs and have the same $h^*$-polynomials. Nevertheless, every matroid of rank two or corank two has a positive $h^*$-coefficient sequence that is ultra log-concave of order equal to the polynomial's degree. In particular, the unimodality conjecture holds in both cases.--------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nEhrhart polynomials of rank two matroids (https://cris.unibo.it/retrieve/8379e6f9-842e-4835-a6b0-5c7c954f260d/1-s2.0-S019688582200094X-main.pdf)\nciteturn1search15 [wordlim: 200] Published: 4.2 years ago; Ehrhart polynomials of rank two matroids\n\nLuis Ferroni*, Katharina Jochemko*, Benjamin Schröter* ... The first of these intensively studied conjectures has recently been disproved by the first author who gave counterexamples in all ranks greater than or equal to three.\nAdvances in Applied Mathematics 141 (2022) 102410\n\nContents lists available at ScienceDirect  \nAdvances in Applied Mathematics  \nwww.elsevier.com/locate/yaama\n\nElsevier\n\nEhrhart polynomials of rank two matroids\n\nLuis Ferroni*, Katharina Jochemko*, Benjamin Schröter*\n\nDepartment of Mathematics, KTH Royal Institute of Technology, Stockholm, Sweden\n\nA R T I C L E   I N F O\n\nArticle history:  \nReceived 28 February 2022  \nAccepted 22 July 2022  \nAvailable online 3 August 2022\n\nMSC:  \n52B40  \n52B20  \n05A15  \n05B35  \n26C10\n\nKeywords:  \nEhrhart theory  \nLattice polytopes  \nMatroids  \nLog-concavity  \nReal-rootedness  \nEhrhart positivity\n\nA B S T R A C T\n\nOver a decade ago De Loera, Haws and Köppe conjectured that Ehrhart polynomials of matroid polytopes have only positive coefficients and that the coefficients of the corresponding h*-polynomials form a unimodal sequence. The first of these intensively studied conjectures has recently been disproved by the first author who gave counterexamples in all ranks greater than or equal to three. --------------------------------------------------------------------------------\nAlma Mater Studiorum - Università di Bologna (https://amsdottorato.unibo.it/9945/1/FerroniRivetti_Luis_tesi.pdf)\nciteturn1search16 [wordlim: 200] Published: 1.4 years ago; Ehrhart polynomials of matroids ... counterexamples to the main Conjectures in 